// 0.0.6 std library: `arr` module — array helpers.
//
// All six helpers are schematic — the single type variable `[T]` is the
// bounded polymorphism of 0.0.3 (resolved per call site by the compiler,
// never spelled by the user). They are VM intrinsics:
//
//     std.arr.len(a)         // -> int
//     std.arr.get(a, i)      // -> Result<T, string>  (Ok(T) in bounds, Err otherwise)
//     std.arr.set(a, i, v)   // -> [T]  (a new array; a is not mutated)
//     std.arr.push(a, v)     // -> [T]  (a new array; a is not mutated)
//     std.arr.concat(a, b)   // -> [T]  (a and b joined; 0.0.6 T07, D5)
//     std.arr.flatten(a)     // -> [T]  (one level of nesting removed; 0.0.6 T07, D5)
//
// Arrays are owned values (D3), so `set`/`push`/`concat`/`flatten` return a
// fresh array rather than mutating their argument; the argument array itself
// is always cloned at the call site. `get` returns a `Result` so the
// out-of-bounds case is a value, not a runtime crash.
//
// `flatten` removes **one** level of nesting only: `flatten(xss)` on
// `[[[T]]]` yields `[[T]]` — chain `flatten` to reach deeper layers
// explicitly (PLAN 0.0.6 D5).
